Dave H. writes:
>[gyugyi@earthsea.stanford.edu (Paul Gyugyi) writes:]
>[] ...
>[] I liked the idea of Stormbringer not asking you if you want
>[] to attack peaceful mosters.  I think Excaliber shouldn't ask
>[] you either -- just not attack.

I do too.  Since this is also a pretty easy change to make to the
code, maybe we can hope to see it in the next version...

>neither of these blades should be wieldable by those not of the
>appropriate alignment, either.

Actually, I think this is a little too draconian.  My 2c worth: have
lawful artifacts refuse to attack any lawful monster except if the
weapon is wielded by a lawful player; I agree that they should never
let you attack a peaceful monster of any sort.  Have chaotic weapons
occasionally attack any monster nearby (including shopkeepers!),
except if wielded by a chaotic player, and have them always suppress
warning messages about peaceful monsters.  Neutral artifacts should
continue to behave as they do now; after all, they are neutral, right?

It might also be interesting if mis-aligned blades occasionally make
you slip, fumble, and/or (rarely) hit yourself:

You slip! -more-
You fumble the black blade! -more-
The black blade cuts you! -more-
You feel weaker.

This would make Stormy extremely dangerous to wield for non-
chaotics... However, as a bonus one might suppose that all artifacts
would be beneficial in helping you to change alignment (to theirs).
In fact, perhaps the very use of an artifact of a different alignment
should gradually misalign you toward it, which would tend to make your
god angry all the time until you either die, convert, or ditch the
weapon.  I can't imagine that any lawful god would be pleased for
their Hand to be running around draining souls...

#pray
You get the feeling that Athena is angry. -more-
A voice booms out: "That weapon offends me, mortal!" -more-
The lightning bolt hits! -more-

